KSH_FOR_BUILD: the Korn shell the build runs, apart from the one it installs.

KSH names the Korn shell of the installed system: it goes into the
scripts' first lines and into programs as KORNSHELL. The build also runs
ksh scripts itself with the same shell: sym2num (dtprintinfo's message
header and the localized catalogues), dtdocbook2sdl (the help volumes,
through $(KSH)) and dtdocbook2man (the manual pages, through its first
line, which names $(KSH)). In a cross build the installed system's ksh is
not on the build machine. configure gets KSH_FOR_BUILD (an environment
variable, default: KSH), and those three runs use it. Without it set
nothing changes.

--- a/configure.ac	2026-11-20 12:03:13
+++ b/configure.ac	2026-11-20 12:03:13
@@ -417,6 +417,11 @@
       fi
    fi
 fi
+
+dnl the Korn shell the build itself runs (sym2num); KSH is the one the
+dnl installed scripts name, which a cross build cannot run
+AC_ARG_VAR([KSH_FOR_BUILD], [Korn shell for scripts run during the build (default: KSH)])
+test -n "$KSH_FOR_BUILD" || KSH_FOR_BUILD=$KSH
 
 AC_PATH_PROG(XRDB, xrdb)
 if test -z "$ac_cv_path_XRDB"; then
